-- title:
--   Theorem 11 — Repeated queries with Markovian noise
-- statement:
--   Work in the setting of §4.3. Vacant auxiliaries and memory $|D\rangle$ are prepared once, the recorded interface is used with ideal root controls, and between queries the algorithm acts arbitrarily on its own registers (address, bus, finite workspace) but never on the retained device. The target is the original database $D$. During each query the device evolves under the time-dependent Markovian generator
--
--   $$\mathcal L_t(\rho)=-i[K,\rho]+\sum_\alpha\mathcal N_{\alpha,t}(\rho),$$
--
--   whose components $\alpha$ have arbitrary supports $S_\alpha$ (record, ports, auxiliaries, memory) and rates $\gamma_\alpha(t)=\|K_{\alpha,t}\|+\sum_\ell\|L_{\alpha\ell,t}\|^2$. Query $j$ occupies the device-time interval $I_j=[(j-1)\tau,j\tau]$, $\tau=\pi/\Omega$. Let $G_j$, $G$ and $\lambda_{\rm dyn}$ be as in (80) and (82), let $F_n=10n^2+14n+12$ be the footprint size, and let $\epsilon_m$ be the diamond distance between the physical $m$-query algorithm (device traced out) and the ideal one. Then:
--
--   1. For every $n\ge1$, $\Omega>0$, real $\Delta$, every noise family with rates integrable over $[0,m\tau]$, every $m\ge1$, database $D$ and algorithm,
--   $$\epsilon_m\le\min\Big\{2,\;8m\sum_{j=1}^m(m-j+1)G_j\Big\}\le\min\{2,\;4m^2(m+1)G\}.$$
--   2. For every $1\le j\le m$,
--   $$G_j\le(F_n+3n+3)\int_{I_j}\lambda_{\rm dyn}(t)\,dt.$$
--   3. For every $\Omega_0>0$ there are constants $C,c>0$, depending only on $\Omega_0$, such that the following holds for every $n\ge1$, every $\Delta$, every $\Omega$ with $\Omega L\ge\Omega_0$ (where $L$ is the schedule length), every such noise family, every $m\ge1$, database and algorithm. If $\lambda_{\rm dyn}(t)\le\gamma$ for $t\in[0,m\tau]$, then $\epsilon_m\le Cm^3\gamma\log^4N$ with $N=2^n$. If every qubit lies in the supports of at most $d\ge1$ components, every rate is at most $\gamma_0$ on $[0,m\tau]$, and $\gamma_0\le c\,\varepsilon/(m^3d\log^4N)$ with $\varepsilon>0$, then $\epsilon_m\le\varepsilon$.
--
--   Compared with static imprecision (Corollary 10), Markovian noise can move the device out of its idle state and back. This return amplitude is what produces the cubic dependence on $m$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The constants of item 3 come from the explicit chain (81)–(82) with $F_n=10n^2+14n+12$, $\tau=\pi/\Omega$ and the schedule length $L$. The noisy evolution is the solution of the integral form of $d\rho/dt=\mathcal L_t(\rho)$. Its existence and uniqueness under integrable rates are part of what is to be proved. Device time is the concatenation of the query intervals.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: one-based bit and transition indices and tensor-product register notation → zero-based finite index types and matrices on all physical bit configurations (the base mission's device model); reason: the finite types enumerate the same modes and every operation is lifted to the full tensor product
--   - encoding: rectangular unhalved diamond norm of the explicitly constructed channels → the identical density-operator supremum over every finite reference dimension for explicit superoperators; reason: same quantity, as in the base mission's Corollary 10
--   - reading: query $j$ is subject to the time-dependent noise on $I_j=[(j-1)\tau,j\tau]$, with root controls and inter-query channels instantaneous and noiseless → device time is the concatenation of the query intervals and zero-based query $k$ runs the noisy propagator from $k\tau$ to $(k+1)\tau$; reason: (80) attaches $I_j$ to query $j$ and §4.3 makes root controls ideal
--   - encoding: the evolution generated by (77) → the solution of its integral form with integrable integrand, chosen from the solution set; reason: under integrable rates the solution exists and is unique, so this is the source's evolution
--   - encoding: the integrals in (80) and the evolution (77) are taken to exist → hypothesis that every rate $\gamma_\alpha$ is interval-integrable on $[0,m\tau]$, and coefficients measurable in $t$; reason: excludes only the cases in which (77) and (80) are undefined
--   - encoding: $G=\max_jG_j$ and $\lambda_{\rm dyn}=\max_q(\cdot)$ → folds of `max` starting at $0$; reason: every $G_j$ and every incidence sum is nonnegative
--   - generalization: the $O(m^3\gamma\log^4N)$ form and the threshold for the construction with $\Omega=\Theta(1/L)$ → for every $\Omega_0>0$ uniform $C,c$ depending only on $\Omega_0$, valid for every $\Omega$ with $\Omega L\ge\Omega_0$ and every real $\Delta$; reason: only the lower side of $\Theta(1/L)$ enters through $\tau=\pi/\Omega$; any source family with $\Omega=\Theta(1/L)$ is an instance
--   - generalization: positive on-site energy splitting $\Delta$ → any real $\Delta$; reason: as in the base mission, the pinning and branch-separation identities used are algebraic in $\Delta$, so every positive splitting is an instance
--   - generalization: $\lambda_{\rm dyn}(t)\le\gamma$ and rates at most $\gamma_0$ for all $t$ → only for $t\in[0,m\tau]$; reason: only the noise during the $m$ query intervals acts; the source's hypotheses imply these
--   - reading: each qubit meets at most $d_{\rm dyn}$ components of rate at most $\gamma_0$ → every component has rate at most $\gamma_0$ and every qubit lies in the supports of at most $d$ components; reason: the plain reading of the sentence, and the one the proof uses
--   - encoding: the per-component fraction $1/d_{\rm dyn}$ → $d\ge1$; reason: division by zero is not a physical precision requirement
--   - reading: (82) for each query interval $I_j$ → for $1\le j\le m$; reason: $G_j$ is attached to the queries $j=1,\dots,m$
-- source:
--   Siddhartha Jain, Alexander Dalzell, Connor Hann, STOC 2027 submission #129 (October 2026), Theorem 11, eqs. (77)–(82), p. 32 (proof p. 33); setting §4.3, p. 30. Canonical PDF SHA256 725dff5ab83fbe7e163da05440b75cc1d24f83804408cc139eb8954d2f8a076e.

import Definitions.Def_ScalablePassiveQRAM_Device_Supplement_Markov
import Definitions.Def_ScalablePassiveQRAM_Device_Energy
import Mathlib

open scoped BigOperators Matrix
set_option synthInstance.maxSize 10000
noncomputable section

namespace ScalablePassiveQRAM.Device.Supplement
open ScalablePassiveQRAM.Device
local instance repeated_queries_markovian_noiseDecidable (p : Prop) : Decidable p :=
  Classical.propDecidable p
local instance repeated_queries_markovian_noiseDecidableEq (a : Type) : DecidableEq a :=
  Classical.decEq a

theorem repeated_queries_markovian_noise :
    (∀ (n : ℕ), 1 ≤ n → ∀ (Ω Δ : ℝ), 0 < Ω → ∀ (N : MarkovNoise n) (m w : ℕ), 1 ≤ m →
      (∀ α, IntervalIntegrable (N.rate α) MeasureTheory.volume 0 ((m : ℝ) * (Real.pi / Ω))) →
      (∀ (D : Database n) (A : Algorithm n w m),
        markovError A N Ω Δ D ≤
          min 2 (8 * m * ∑ j ∈ Finset.Icc 1 m, ((m : ℝ) - j + 1) * N.exposure Ω j)) ∧
      min 2 (8 * m * ∑ j ∈ Finset.Icc 1 m, ((m : ℝ) - j + 1) * N.exposure Ω j) ≤
        min 2 (4 * m ^ 2 * (m + 1) * N.maxExposure Ω m) ∧
      (∀ j : ℕ, 1 ≤ j → j ≤ m →
        N.exposure Ω j ≤ (footprintSize n + 3 * n + 3) *
          ∫ t in ((j : ℝ) - 1) * (Real.pi / Ω)..(j : ℝ) * (Real.pi / Ω), N.dynIncidence t)) ∧
    (∀ Ω₀ : ℝ, 0 < Ω₀ → ∃ C c : ℝ, 0 < C ∧ 0 < c ∧ ∀ (n : ℕ), 1 ≤ n →
      ∀ (Ω Δ : ℝ), Ω₀ ≤ Ω * (length n : ℝ) →
      ∀ (N : MarkovNoise n) (m w : ℕ), 1 ≤ m →
      (∀ α, IntervalIntegrable (N.rate α) MeasureTheory.volume 0 ((m : ℝ) * (Real.pi / Ω))) →
      ∀ (D : Database n) (A : Algorithm n w m),
        (∀ γ : ℝ, (∀ t ∈ Set.Icc 0 ((m : ℝ) * (Real.pi / Ω)), N.dynIncidence t ≤ γ) →
          markovError A N Ω Δ D ≤ C * m ^ 3 * γ * (Real.log ((2 : ℝ) ^ n)) ^ 4) ∧
        (∀ (d : ℕ) (γ₀ ε : ℝ), 1 ≤ d → 0 < ε →
          (∀ q : ExtendedQubit n, (Finset.univ.filter fun α => q ∈ N.support α).card ≤ d) →
          (∀ α, ∀ t ∈ Set.Icc 0 ((m : ℝ) * (Real.pi / Ω)), N.rate α t ≤ γ₀) →
          γ₀ ≤ c * ε / (m ^ 3 * d * (Real.log ((2 : ℝ) ^ n)) ^ 4) →
          markovError A N Ω Δ D ≤ ε)) := by sorry

end ScalablePassiveQRAM.Device.Supplement
